# Buildroom discovery and name routing

Keep this block alongside the target repository's existing instructions; it does not replace them.

Start Buildroom discovery by reading `buildroom/knowledge/catalog.yml`. Use its IDs, tags, status, authority, and relationships to choose a suitable role or workflow without scanning the full directory. A selected workflow's `context` field names its generated `buildroom/knowledge/context/...` pack; read that pack in the listed order, then follow the canonical files it links.

When the user names a Buildroom agent or workflow (for example `Researcher`, `PM`, `QA`, `Architect`, `bug fix`, or `feature delivery`), resolve the human-friendly name through `buildroom/registry.tsv` before asking for a Markdown path. Use `sh buildroom/bin/buildroom resolve "<name>"` when a link or exact prompt is useful. State the canonical name and link to the resolved file, then read that file before acting.

For an initialized repository, open a durable run explicitly with `sh buildroom/bin/buildroom start <workflow-name-or-id> <work-id> [--project <path>]`; the express No-Bullshit Bug Fix route also requires `--approve-express`. Inspect one run with `sh buildroom/bin/buildroom check <work-id> [--project <path>]`. A check is read-only and reports structural readiness only: it does not grant a human approval, accept a hand-off, or advance workflow ownership.

The catalog is authoritative for Buildroom metadata; the registry is authoritative only for human-friendly names. An unknown or ambiguous name must not be guessed: run `sh buildroom/bin/buildroom list`, show the recognised names, and ask the user to choose. If the registry is unavailable or the user prefers not to choose a recognised name, do not block the task: ask for an explicit `buildroom/...` Markdown path, then read that path before acting. Existing repository instructions, approval gates, and coding standards remain authoritative.
